## 1. Executive Summary: Demystifying the Disaggregation Hype

> **Why this section is here:**  
> Industry whitepapers frequently promote Prefill/Decode Disaggregation as an all-around performance booster. This section immediately establishes our core thesis based on empirical ground-truth: **Disaggregation is an SLA-protection architecture, NOT a raw throughput or TTFT booster.** Collocated serving often outperforms disaggregation under typical traffic, and disaggregation only justifies its hardware cost under specific queue-saturation thresholds.

### Key Empirical Findings:
1. **Collocated Wins on Raw Throughput and TTFT:** Under decode-heavy and balanced workloads, Collocated serving achieves up to **$25\%$ higher throughput** and **$2\times$ to $3\times$ lower Time-To-First-Token (TTFT)** due to $100\%$ dynamic resource pooling and zero KV-cache network serialization overhead.
2. **The Operational Saturation Threshold:** Under open-loop Poisson arrival traffic ($\lambda \le 4.3\text{ req/s}$), Collocated serving with chunked prefill easily absorbs prompt arrivals. Disaggregation provides virtually zero ITL benefit ($\sim 3\text{ ms}$) while severely penalizing TTFT.
3. **The Single Metric Disaggregation Wins: Tail-Latency (P99 ITL) Stability:** When the cluster is pushed into sustained concurrency ($C \ge 4$ on 8k prompts) or sudden burst shockwaves, Collocated serving suffers from severe **Head-of-Line (HoL) blocking**, blowing out P99 ITL to **$80\text{ ms} - 122\text{ ms}$**. Disaggregation physically isolates the decode loop, keeping P99 ITL bounded at **$13\text{ ms} - 20\text{ ms}$**.
4. **The Interconnect Tax:** Transferring an 8k prompt KV cache ($1.15\text{ GB}$) across **100G InfiniBand** adds **$+927.7\text{ ms}$** of network serialization latency and causes **$73.5\%$ CUDA driver stall time** (`cuStreamSynchronize` / `cuMemcpyAsync`). Over **NVLink ($600\text{ GB/s}$)**, the transfer consumes only $1.1\%$ of stream execution time.

