## 4. Macro Evaluation I: The Scaling Paradox ($N \le 3$ GPUs)

> **Why this section is here:**  
> It is widely assumed that disaggregating 2 or 3 GPUs is strictly better than collocated serving. This section proves that at small scale ($N \le 3$), Disaggregation creates an artificial **Single-Prefill Bottleneck** under long-prompt workloads.

### Empirical Comparison: 2 GPUs (Collocated 2x TP1 vs. Disaggregated 1P:1D)

Under Enterprise RAG ($8192\text{ in} \times 128\text{ out}$):

| Concurrency | Architecture | Throughput (out tok/s) | Mean TTFT (ms) | P99 TTFT (ms) | P99 ITL (ms) | Architectural State |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **$C=1$** | Collocated (2x TP1) | **87.2 tok/s** | **447.8 ms** | **456.9 ms** | 15.3 ms | Both GPUs serve independently |
| **$C=1$** | Disaggregated (1P:1D) | 43.1 tok/s | 1145.4 ms | 1184.4 ms | **14.8 ms** | 1P GPU active, 1D GPU starved |
| **$C=4$** | Collocated (2x TP1) | **333.1 tok/s** | **338.4 ms** | **787.2 ms** | 148.9 ms | Saturated, high ITL jitter |
| **$C=4$** | Disaggregated (1P:1D) | 134.4 tok/s | 1856.7 ms | 2841.5 ms | **15.8 ms** | **1P Bottleneck:** Decoder starved 60% of time |
| **$C=16$**| Collocated (2x TP1) | **487.6 tok/s** | **1242.0 ms** | **6891.2 ms** | 179.3 ms | Full capacity, severe HoL blocking |
| **$C=16$**| Disaggregated (1P:1D) | 138.9 tok/s | 5849.2 ms | 18452.1 ms| **16.1 ms** | **Throughput collapsed:** Massive prefill queue |

### Systems Analysis:
* **The Single-P Bottleneck:** In 1P:1D, only 1 GPU is available to process 8,192-token prompt matrices. Because prefill takes $\sim 60\text{ ms}$ per request, the prefill queue backs up. The single decode GPU spends over half its time idle waiting for KV-caches to arrive.
* **The Trade-off:** Collocated $2\times\text{TP1}$ delivers **$3.5\times$ more throughput** at $C=16$, but suffers from a **$179.3\text{ ms}$ P99 ITL**. Disaggregation successfully bounds P99 ITL at **$16.1\text{ ms}$**, but sacrifices $71\%$ of the cluster's potential throughput.

